# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Vera — Environment Overrides
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Copy to .env and adjust values. docker compose reads this
#  automatically. All values shown are the defaults.

# ── Host port mappings (left side of host:container) ─────
# ORCHESTRATOR_PORT=8999
# REDIS_PORT=6379
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433
# CHROMA_PORT=8008
# NEO4J_HTTP_PORT=7474
# NEO4J_BOLT_PORT=7687
# GARAGE_S3_PORT=3900
# GARAGE_ADMIN_PORT=3903
# BUILDER_PORT=8785
# VSCODE_PORT=8843

# ── Build / compile service (vera-builder) ──────────────
#  arduino-cli + ESP32 core, PlatformIO, gcc/cmake, esptool, mpy-cross.
#  In-stack orchestrator reaches it by service name; a NATIVE (non-docker)
#  orchestrator should point at the published port, e.g.
#    VERA_BUILDER_URL=http://localhost:8785
# VERA_BUILDER_URL=http://vera-builder:8080
#  Default board for mesh.firmware.build / build.arduino when none is given:
# BUILDER_DEFAULT_FQBN=esp32:esp32:esp32

# ── Central VS Code (code-server) ───────────────────────
#  The IDE tab's primary surface. Login password for the vscode service —
#  the same value is passed to the orchestrator so the IDE header 🔑 /
#  Workers → Connections can copy/reveal it. Rotate from the UI
#  (ide.vscode.password.set) or change it here and recreate the service.
# VSCODE_PASSWORD=vera-code
#  Where the orchestrator reaches code-server (proxied same-origin at
#  /vscode/central/). NATIVE (non-docker) orchestrator: point at the
#  published port instead, e.g. http://localhost:8843
# VSCODE_CENTRAL_URL=http://vscode:8080
#  Volume holding the shared projects tree, as seen by the docker daemon
#  (compose prefixes it with the project name):
# VSCODE_PROJECTS_VOLUME=vera_vera-projects
#  Comma-sep extensions installed by ide.vscode.central.ensure (open-vsx ids):
# VSCODE_CENTRAL_EXTENSIONS=
#  Image + port-base for sandbox "interactive worker" sidecars:
# VSCODE_WORKER_IMAGE=codercom/code-server:latest
# VSCODE_WORKER_PORT_BASE=8860

# ── Garage admin token ──────────────────────────────────
#  Used by the garage-init bootstrap sidecar and by
#  provision.store.garage.bootstrap. MUST match
#  [admin].admin_token in garage/garage.toml.
# GARAGE_ADMIN_TOKEN=vera-garage-admin-change-me

# ── Neo4j credentials ───────────────────────────────────
# NEO4J_USER=neo4j
# NEO4J_PASS=veraneo4j

# ── Ollama cluster (external hosts) ─────────────────────
# OLLAMA_GPU_URL=http://192.168.0.250:11435
# OLLAMA_CPU_A_URL=http://192.168.0.246:11435
# OLLAMA_CPU_B_URL=http://192.168.0.247:11435
# OLLAMA_MODEL=mistral
# OLLAMA_EMBED_URL=http://192.168.0.246:11435
# OLLAMA_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# Generation STALL timeout in seconds (default 900): requests stream from
# Ollama, so this is the max silence BETWEEN chunks (+ connect/prompt-eval),
# not a cap on total generation time. Only a request producing nothing fails.
# OLLAMA_GEN_TIMEOUT=900
# Optional cap on time queued waiting for a node's generation slot.
# Default 0 = wait indefinitely (queueing is progress, not failure; liveness
# heartbeats keep queued jobs from being flagged stale).
# OLLAMA_QUEUE_TIMEOUT=0
# Embedding HTTP timeout (default 300). Generous: Ollama serialises per node,
# so an embed may legitimately wait behind a running generation.
# OLLAMA_EMBED_TIMEOUT=300
# How long a node keeps the model resident after a call (default 10m)
# OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=10m

# ── GPU inference (Whisper / TTS / SD) ──────────────────
# GPU_INFER_URL=http://192.168.0.250:8765
# When Stable Diffusion OOMs on CUDA (an LLM is resident on the card), the GPU
# server unloads the co-located Ollama model(s) to reclaim VRAM and retries on
# CUDA before falling back to CPU. In-flight generations are never interrupted
# (keep_alive:0 defers the unload); a live generation is detected (VRAM stays
# held) and that image renders on CPU instead. Set to 0 to disable entirely so
# image generation never disturbs LLM residency. (read by edge/GPU_inference.py)
# SD_EVICT_OLLAMA_ON_OOM=1

# ── Backend network hostname ────────────────────────────
#  Single internal-domain knob. Host-derived defaults (SearXNG, research data
#  sources, the Google OAuth redirect base, etc.) build on it, and it is injected
#  into the served UI (window.__VERA_DOMAIN__ / window.__VERA_BASE__) so the
#  browser panels resolve the backend from config too. Set it once to retarget.
# BACKEND_HOST=llm.int

# ── HTTPS / TLS ─────────────────────────────────────────
#  Browsers only expose secure-context features — the Web Serial API
#  (navigator.serial) and getUserMedia (webcam/mic) — over HTTPS or to
#  localhost. Set TLS_ENABLED=1 to serve https:// on the same ORCHESTRATOR_PORT.
#  A self-signed cert is auto-generated at ~/.vera/tls on first start (browsers
#  show a one-time "not trusted" warning). Honoured by both docker compose AND
#  native runs (./build.sh run / make run). Point TLS_CERTFILE/TLS_KEYFILE at
#  your own PEM files to supply a real cert instead.
# TLS_ENABLED=1
# TLS_CERTFILE=~/.vera/tls/cert.pem
# TLS_KEYFILE=~/.vera/tls/key.pem

# ── Google OAuth (calendar) ─────────────────────────────
#  The calendar module's Google Calendar sync uses an OAuth redirect: after you
#  approve, Google sends the browser back to Vera at
#  <base>/cal/google/oauth_callback and the refresh token is captured
#  automatically (the old paste-the-code flow is gone).
#  Defaults to http(s)://<BACKEND_HOST>:<ORCHESTRATOR_PORT> (e.g.
#  http://llm.int:8999) so it works from any machine on the LAN — for this,
#  create a Google Cloud OAuth client of type "Web application" and register the
#  exact <base>/cal/google/oauth_callback URI there. TLS is strongly recommended
#  (set TLS_ENABLED=1) since the token rides this redirect.
#  Alternatively, if you always open Vera on the same host it runs on, set a
#  loopback base and use a "Desktop app" client (accepts any loopback port):
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE=https://llm.int:8999
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE=http://localhost:8999

# ── Data-fabric object store (Garage S3) ────────────────
#  Set FABRIC_OBJECT_STORE=none to disable blob storage entirely.
#  ACCESS/SECRET are deterministic dev creds shared by the vera and
#  garage-init services — set them here once to keep both in sync.
#  Generate real ones with:
#    ACCESS: printf 'GK%s' "$(openssl rand -hex 12)"
#    SECRET: openssl rand -hex 32
#  FABRIC_S3_ENDPOINT is fixed to the in-compose garage service and
#  is not overridable here. If you change FABRIC_S3_REGION, update
#  s3_region in garage/garage.toml to match (SigV4 region check).
# FABRIC_OBJECT_STORE=garage
# FABRIC_S3_ACCESS=GKa1b2c3d4e5f6708192a3b4c5
# FABRIC_S3_SECRET=a1b2c3d4e5f60718293a4b5c6d7e8f90a1b2c3d4e5f60718293a4b5c6d7e8f90
# FABRIC_S3_BUCKET=vera-data-fabric
# FABRIC_S3_REGION=garage
